  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/f03d1f23687273ff94bb2ea4ee45991600c75a237bf4c0a1fdb955d0f00b9b3d.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={f}^{\u2033}(x)$ of the function $f(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-2$ and $x=3,$ as shown above. Find the interval on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down.\n\n$f(x)$  is concave down on the interval\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{\u2033}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFrom the plotted graph of $y={f}^{\u2033}(x)$, we can  see that ${f}^{\u2033}(x)<0$ on the interval   $(-2,3)$ \n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave down on the interval $(\\,-2,3\\,).$",
